\chapter{Results}
\label{ch:results}

This chapter reports the experimental results of the proposed Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning (MARL) DQN traffic signal controller, benchmarked against four classical baselines on the Cologne OSM network under moderate congestion (demand scale~$=0.7$). Following established practice in the traffic-signal-control literature, results are presented as a structured multi-metric comparison covering traffic efficiency, environmental impact, and composite performance indicators \cite{noaeen2022reinforcement, xiao2024advances}.

Prior work stresses that the credibility of learning-based traffic control depends on \emph{(i)} comparison against multiple baselines under identical demand, \emph{(ii)} reporting both efficiency and robustness, and \emph{(iii)} relating each metric to the intended control objective \cite{xiao2024advances,noaeen2022reinforcement}. Accordingly, this evaluation reports core traffic metrics (waiting time, queue length, throughput, congestion), environmental metrics (CO$_2$), and derived composite metrics (throughput efficiency, waiting time per arrived vehicle) that reveal where the learned policy adds practical value beyond what simple rules achieve.

The remainder of this chapter is organized as follows: Section~\ref{sec:results_setup} summarizes the evaluation and training setup; Section~\ref{sec:results_main} presents the main comparative results; Section~\ref{sec:results_derived} analyses derived efficiency metrics; Section~\ref{sec:results_stat} provides statistical significance testing; Section~\ref{sec:results_training} discusses training dynamics; and Section~\ref{sec:results_discussion} interprets the findings and their limitations.


