\subsection{Analysis of Core Metrics}

\textbf{Throughput.} The MARL DQN controller achieves the highest mean throughput of $775.8$\,veh/h, representing a $+12.0\%$ improvement over Fixed-Time ($692.4$\,veh/h). This exceeds all classical baselines: SOTL ($+9.6\%$), Adaptive ($+10.1\%$), and MaxPressure ($+11.8\%$). However, the absolute gap over MaxPressure is small ($+1.9$\,veh/h), so the advantage should be interpreted as incremental rather than transformative.

\textbf{Congestion index.} The clearest advantage of the MARL agent appears in the congestion index (fraction of time-lane samples experiencing queuing). MARL DQN achieves $0.64$, a $-13.7\%$ reduction versus Fixed-Time. This outperforms all baselines: SOTL ($-2.4\%$), Adaptive ($-7.5\%$), and MaxPressure ($-11.1\%$). Relative to MaxPressure, the reduction is approximately $2.6$ percentage points. This pattern is consistent with the interpretation that the learned policy allocates green time in a way that suppresses localized queue buildup more effectively than the rule-based alternatives.

\textbf{Average waiting time and queue length.} All adaptive controllers reduce average waiting time relative to Fixed-Time, but the improvements are modest ($< 2\%$). SOTL achieves the largest waiting-time reduction ($-1.2\%$), while MARL DQN ($-0.6\%$) and Adaptive ($-0.7\%$) are comparable. Average queue length reductions follow a similar pattern, with SOTL ($-1.5\%$) and MARL DQN ($-1.4\%$) leading. This reflects the fact that on a large city network with only 50\% of intersections controlled, the network-wide average delay is dominated by uncontrolled intersections, compressing the observable differences.

\textbf{Average speed.} All adaptive controllers improve average vehicle speed by approximately $4\%$ over Fixed-Time ($0.61$\,m/s). SOTL achieves the largest speed gain ($+4.6\%$), while MARL DQN achieves $+3.9\%$, reflecting a trade-off between congestion reduction and throughput maximization.

