The throughput efficiency metric reveals the clearest advantage of the MARL DQN controller: a $+29.6\%$ improvement over Fixed-Time, compared to $+25.6\%$ for MaxPressure and $+12.3\%$ for SOTL. The MARL agent processes nearly 1\,195 vehicles per hour per unit of congestion, versus 1\,158 for MaxPressure---a $3.2\%$ relative improvement. This arises because the MARL agent simultaneously attains the highest throughput and the lowest congestion index among the evaluated strategies.

The normalized burden metrics also favor the MARL agent. The normalized waiting burden ($119\,355$\,s per arrived vehicle) is the lowest among the evaluated controllers, while CO$_2$ per arrived vehicle is tied with MaxPressure at $2.42 \times 10^7$\,mg. These results suggest that the throughput gains do not come at the expense of a higher normalized waiting or emissions burden.

