\section{Statistical Significance}
\label{sec:results_stat}

To assess whether the observed differences are statistically reliable, pairwise Welch's $t$-tests are performed between the MARL DQN agent and each baseline. Table~\ref{tab:stat_tests} reports the test statistics and $p$-values for MARL DQN versus SOTL (the strongest traditional adaptive baseline in terms of waiting time reduction).

\begin{table}[!htbp]
    \centering
    \caption{Welch's $t$-test: MARL DQN vs.\ SOTL ($n = 120$ episodes each).}
    \label{tab:stat_tests}
    \begin{tabular}{l r r l}
        \hline
        \textbf{Metric} & \textbf{$t$-statistic} & \textbf{$p$-value} & \textbf{Significance} \\
        \hline
        Avg waiting time      & $3.34$   & $9.81 \times 10^{-4}$   & $***$ \\
        Avg queue length      & $0.74$   & $0.461$                  & n.s. \\
        Throughput (/h)       & $3.24$   & $1.36 \times 10^{-3}$   & $**$ \\
        Congestion index      & $-38.19$ & $8.33 \times 10^{-102}$ & $***$ \\
        Avg speed             & $-2.97$  & $3.26 \times 10^{-3}$   & $**$ \\
        Total CO$_2$          & $3.66$   & $3.15 \times 10^{-4}$   & $***$ \\
        Throughput efficiency  & $17.99$  & $1.61 \times 10^{-45}$  & $***$ \\
        \hline
        \multicolumn{4}{l}{\footnotesize Significance codes: $***$\;$p < 0.001$,\; $**$\;$p < 0.01$,\; $*$\;$p < 0.05$,\; n.s.\;not significant.}
    \end{tabular}
\end{table}

The congestion index difference between MARL DQN and SOTL is overwhelmingly significant ($t = -38.19$, $p < 10^{-100}$), indicating substantially lower congestion under the MARL policy. Throughput efficiency is likewise highly significant ($t = 17.99$, $p < 10^{-45}$), showing that the composite advantage is robust to episode-level stochasticity. Throughput is significantly higher for MARL DQN ($p < 0.01$). By contrast, the average waiting-time comparison is also statistically significant but directionally favors SOTL, which attains a slightly lower mean waiting time in this setting (94.80\,s versus 95.34\,s). The only non-significant comparison is average queue length ($p = 0.461$), where MARL DQN and SOTL perform similarly. The total CO$_2$ comparison is statistically significant, but the absolute difference is practically small and should therefore be interpreted cautiously.

