\section{Training Analysis}
\label{sec:results_training}

This section examines the training dynamics of the MARL DQN agent over 300~episodes on the Cologne network (demand scale 0.7). The reported reward curve should be interpreted as an optimization signal rather than a direct traffic-performance metric, because the reward combines waiting-time penalties, queue-change terms, switching penalties, and regional shaping.

\subsection{Reward Convergence}
Figure~\ref{fig:training_reward} shows the per-episode training reward. The reward improves during the early part of training as the agent moves from highly exploratory behaviour ($\varepsilon \approx 1.0$) toward more exploitative behaviour, and then exhibits a comparatively stable regime. The smoothed trend is therefore used as evidence of learning stability, rather than as a standalone claim about network-level optimality.

