\subsection{Loss Convergence}
Figure~\ref{fig:training_loss} presents the per-episode training loss (Huber loss / Smooth L1). After an initial transient phase, the loss decreases and enters a relatively stable range, indicating that the Q-network is fitting its Bellman targets more consistently. As is standard in deep reinforcement learning, a lower TD loss should not be interpreted on its own as proof of better traffic performance, but rather as supporting evidence of numerical training stability. The stabilization techniques (hard target updates every 2\,000 steps, Q-target clamping, gradient clipping, and Layer Normalization) likely contribute to preventing the divergence observed in earlier model iterations without these safeguards.

