\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:results_discussion}

\subsection{Where the MARL Agent Excels}
The MARL DQN agent achieves the best performance on two key dimensions:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Congestion index} ($-13.7\%$ vs Fixed-Time): the largest improvement among all controllers, indicating that the learned policy reduces the proportion of time that lanes experience queuing. This is statistically highly significant ($p < 10^{-100}$).
    \item \textbf{Throughput efficiency} ($+29.6\%$ vs Fixed-Time): the MARL agent simultaneously maximizes throughput and minimizes congestion, achieving the highest composite score. This is also highly significant ($p < 10^{-45}$).
\end{enumerate}

These findings are consistent with the interpretation that the MARL agent learns a signal-timing strategy that distributes green time more effectively across competing phases, thereby reducing the queue-accumulation patterns that degrade network performance under rule-based controllers.

\subsection{Where Improvements Are Modest}
Average waiting time and average queue length improvements are below $2\%$ for all controllers relative to Fixed-Time. This is primarily because:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Only 50\% of the network's intersections are controlled; the remaining intersections contribute substantially to aggregate delay.
    \item Waiting time is a network-wide average that includes vehicles never routed through controlled intersections.
    \item On the Cologne network at moderate demand, the absolute delay differences are compressed by the large number of uncontrolled intersections.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Comparison with Classical Baselines}
Among classical baselines, MaxPressure emerges as the strongest competitor, achieving the second-best throughput ($+11.8\%$) and congestion index ($-11.1\%$). The gap between MARL DQN and MaxPressure is small on individual metrics but consistent across all throughput and congestion measures, and the composite throughput efficiency metric reveals a clearer separation ($1\,195$ vs $1\,158$, $+3.2\%$).

SOTL achieves the best waiting-time reduction ($-1.2\%$) among classical baselines, while Adaptive provides a balanced middle ground. The diversity of baseline strengths underscores the value of reporting multiple metrics rather than a single objective.

\subsection{Limitations}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Partial control}: only 50\% of intersections are controlled, which compresses observable differences in network-wide metrics. Controlling a larger fraction could amplify the MARL agent's advantage.
    \item \textbf{Single network}: results are reported on one city network (Cologne) at one demand level (scale 0.7). Generalization to other topologies and demand patterns requires further evaluation.
    \item \textbf{Training-seed variance}: the comparative evaluation averages over many SUMO seeds, but the learned policy itself is represented by one trained checkpoint per configuration. Variability across independent training runs was not exhaustively quantified.
    \item \textbf{Computational cost}: training the MARL DQN agent for 300~episodes requires significantly more computation than deploying any of the classical baselines, which require no training phase. However, the trained policy has the same inference cost as rule-based controllers at deployment time.
    \item \textbf{Environmental impact}: the MARL agent does not reduce total CO$_2$ emissions, as these are dominated by the uncontrolled portion of the network. Per-vehicle emission reductions are achieved indirectly through higher throughput.
\end{itemize}

